                <h1 class="content-title">Procurement, Sourcing &amp; <span class="gradient-text">Receiving Mechanics</span></h1>
                <p class="content-subtitle">
                    Follow the capital lifecycle: Discover how user catalog requests trigger automated sourcing evaluation, Purchase Order execution, and automated asset generation at the loading dock.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- 1. Service Catalog Pipeline -->
            <section class="content-section" id="catalog-pipeline">
                <h2>1. The Service Catalog Sourcing Pipeline</h2>
                <p>
                    In an enterprise running ServiceNow HAM Pro, hardware acquisition rarely starts as an ad-hoc purchase. It begins when an employee, project manager, or automated onboarding workflow requests hardware through the <strong>Service Catalog</strong>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The catalog request progresses through a 3-tier transactional hierarchy:
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Request (<code>sc_request</code>):</strong> The shopping cart container capturing total financial approval, requester information, and billing cost center.</li>
                    <li><strong>Requested Item (<code>sc_req_item</code> / RITM):</strong> The specific hardware item requested (e.g., "Developer Laptop Bundle: Apple MacBook Pro 16 + Dual Dell 4K Monitors").</li>
                    <li><strong>Catalog Task (<code>sc_task</code>):</strong> The fulfillment instructions for technical teams (e.g., provisioning, asset tagging, imaging).</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="diagram-block" style="margin: 28px 0;">
                    <div class="diagram-title">End-to-End Procurement, Sourcing &amp; Asset Creation Flow</div>
                    <div class="mermaid">
                    graph TD
                        Req["Catalog Request<br/>(sc_request / sc_req_item)"] --> Approval{"Financial Approval<br/>Manager / Cost Center"}
                        Approval -->|Approved| Sourcing["Sourcing Engine<br/>clm_sourcing"]
                        
                        Sourcing --> StockCheck{"Local Stockroom<br/>Available?"}
                        StockCheck -->|Yes| LocalPick["Reserve and Generate<br/>Warehouse Pick Task"]
                        
                        StockCheck -->|No| RegionalCheck{"Regional Hub<br/>Available?"}
                        RegionalCheck -->|Yes| GenTO["Generate Transfer Order<br/>(alm_transfer_order)"]
                        
                        RegionalCheck -->|No| GenPO["Generate Purchase Order<br/>(proc_po / proc_po_item)"]
                        GenPO --> Vendor["Send EDI / API to Vendor<br/>(Dell / Apple / CDW)"]
                        
                        Vendor --> Ship["Freight Shipment In Transit<br/>(ASN Ingested)"]
                        Ship --> Dock["Loading Dock Arrival<br/>Receiving Slip (proc_rec_slip)"]
                        
                        Dock --> AutoAsset["Automated Asset Generation<br/>alm_hardware created"]
                        AutoAsset --> Link["Asset Linked to RITM and Reserved<br/>State: In Stock / Reserved"]
                        
                        classDef default fill:#161b26,stroke:#334155,stroke-width:1px,color:#f8fafc
                        classDef highlight fill:#161b26,stroke:#10b981,stroke-width:2px,color:#f8fafc
                        classDef blue fill:#161b26,stroke:#3b82f6,stroke-width:2px,color:#f8fafc
                        classDef amber fill:#161b26,stroke:#f59e0b,stroke-width:2px,color:#f8fafc
                        class Sourcing,AutoAsset highlight
                        class GenPO,GenTO blue
                        class StockCheck,RegionalCheck amber
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 2. Sourcing Engine Decisions -->
            <section class="content-section" id="sourcing-engine">
                <h2>2. Sourcing Engine Decisions</h2>
                <p>
                    When a catalog item is configured with a valid hardware model (<code>cmdb_hardware_product_model</code>) and the <code>Sourceable</code> flag is true, ServiceNow activates the <strong>Sourcing Engine</strong>. Instead of blindly issuing purchase orders and wasting budget, the engine evaluates existing enterprise stock across three operational pathways:
                </p>

                <div class="grid-3" style="margin-top: 20px;">
                    <div class="card-basic">
                        <div style="color: var(--accent-primary); font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px;">Path 1: Local Allocation</div>
                        <h4>Immediate Stock Fulfillment</h4>
                        <p>
                            If an asset of the requested model is currently sitting in the requester's local office stockroom in state <code>In Stock / Available</code>, the system immediately reserves it:
                        </p>
                        <ul class="feature-bullet-list">
                            <li>Asset state transitions to <code>In Stock / Reserved</code>.</li>
                            <li>Binds <code>alm_hardware.request_line</code> directly to the RITM.</li>
                            <li>Generates a local stockroom staging/picking task.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <div class="card-basic">
                        <div style="color: var(--accent-secondary); font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px;">Path 2: Transfer Order</div>
                        <h4>Inter-Stockroom Logistics</h4>
                        <p>
                            If the local stockroom is depleted but a regional warehouse has surplus inventory, the engine generates an <strong>Automated Transfer Order</strong>:
                        </p>
                        <ul class="feature-bullet-list">
                            <li>Selects the nearest warehouse based on location hierarchy.</li>
                            <li>Reserves the asset at the source stockroom.</li>
                            <li>Initiates shipping prep and freight carrier tracking.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <div class="card-basic">
                        <div style="color: var(--accent-tertiary); font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px;">Path 3: Vendor Procurement</div>
                        <h4>Purchase Order Generation</h4>
                        <p>
                            When enterprise-wide inventory is exhausted, the engine initiates vendor procurement:
                        </p>
                        <ul class="feature-bullet-list">
                            <li>Combines like requests into batch PO lines.</li>
                            <li>Applies negotiated vendor contract discount tiers.</li>
                            <li>Routes for secondary financial authorization if PO exceeds threshold.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 3. Purchase Orders & Lines -->
            <section class="content-section" id="po-lifecycle">
                <h2>3. Purchase Orders &amp; Line Items</h2>
                <p>
                    A <strong>Purchase Order (PO)</strong> is a legally binding contract sent to a hardware manufacturer or distributor. In ServiceNow, procurement is structured into header records and granular line items.
                </p>

                <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top: 20px;">
                    <div class="card-basic">
                        <h3>Purchase Order (<code>proc_po</code>)</h3>
                        <p>
                            The parent commercial instrument containing enterprise procurement metadata:
                        </p>
                        <ul class="feature-bullet-list">
                            <li><strong>PO Number:</strong> Standard tracking string (e.g., <code>PO100482</code>).</li>
                            <li><strong>Vendor:</strong> Master company record from <code>core_company</code> (e.g., Dell Direct).</li>
                            <li><strong>Ship-to Stockroom:</strong> Target delivery warehouse (<code>alm_stockroom</code>).</li>
                            <li><strong>Total Amount:</strong> Aggregated sum of all child PO lines.</li>
                            <li><strong>Status:</strong> Requested, Approved, Ordered, Received, Canceled.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <div class="card-basic">
                        <h3>PO Line Item (<code>proc_po_item</code>)</h3>
                        <p>
                            The atomic line item specifying exact hardware items, quantities, and cost centers:
                        </p>
                        <ul class="feature-bullet-list">
                            <li><strong>Model:</strong> References <code>cmdb_hardware_product_model</code>.</li>
                            <li><strong>Vendor Part Number:</strong> Manufacturer SKU identifier.</li>
                            <li><strong>Ordered Quantity:</strong> Total units purchased.</li>
                            <li><strong>Unit Cost:</strong> Contracted unit purchase price.</li>
                            <li><strong>Cost Center / GL Account:</strong> General ledger budget code for ERP reconciliation (SAP/Oracle Financials).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 4. Receiving Slips & Creation -->
            <section class="content-section" id="receiving-mechanics">
                <h2>4. Receiving Slips &amp; Automated Asset Generation</h2>
                <p>
                    When delivery trucks arrive at an enterprise loading dock, warehouse technicians process shipments through the <strong>Receiving</strong> interface. In HAM Pro, receiving is where financial promises translate into physical tracked records.
                </p>

                <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top: 20px;">
                    <div class="card-basic">
                        <h4>Receiving Slip (<code>proc_rec_slip</code>)</h4>
                        <p>
                            Represents the physical delivery event. Tracks the carrier tracking number, packing slip invoice number, receiving stockroom, and receiving technician timestamp.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card-basic">
                        <h4>Receiving Slip Item (<code>proc_rec_slip_item</code>)</h4>
                        <p>
                            Child records detailing the quantity received against each specific PO line. Automatically handles <strong>partial shipments</strong> (e.g., 50 servers ordered on Line 1; 30 received on Monday, 20 on Wednesday).
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-card info-card--important" style="margin-top: 24px;">
                    <div class="info-card-title">The Automated Asset Creation Trigger</div>
                    <p>
                        When a technician clicks <strong>Receive</strong> on a Receiving Slip Line, ServiceNow executes the core receiving engine:
                    </p>
                    <ol style="margin: 8px 0 0 20px; line-height: 1.8;">
                        <li>It reads the ordered quantity and model category. If the category is serialized hardware (<code>alm_hardware</code>), it immediately inserts $N$ separate records in <code>alm_hardware</code>.</li>
                        <li>It automatically applies the stockroom location from the receiving slip header.</li>
                        <li>It sets the initial Asset State to <code>In Stock</code> and Substate to <code>Available</code> (or <code>Reserved</code> if pre-allocated to an RITM).</li>
                        <li>It sets the Asset <code>cost</code> equal to the unit cost from the PO line, permanently recording acquisition basis for financial depreciation.</li>
                        <li>It immediately fires the <code>AssetAndCISynchronizer</code> to generate corresponding records in <code>cmdb_ci_hardware</code>.</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 5. ASN Manifest Ingestion -->
            <section class="content-section" id="asn-edi">
                <h2>5. Advanced Shipment Notification (ASN) &amp; EDI Ingestion</h2>
                <p>
                    In mature enterprise environments, waiting for physical boxes to arrive before entering data into ServiceNow introduces bottlenecks. Top-tier hardware vendors (Dell, HP, Lenovo, Apple) provide automated <strong>Advanced Shipment Notifications (ASN / EDI 856)</strong>.
                </p>
                <div class="data-table-wrapper" style="margin-top: 16px;">
                    <table class="data-table">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Procurement Milestone</th>
                                <th>Data Ingestion Mechanism</th>
                                <th>Asset State in ServiceNow</th>
                                <th>Business Benefit</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>PO Placed</strong></td>
                                <td>ServiceNow IntegrationHub EDI 850</td>
                                <td><em>None (PO Line created)</em></td>
                                <td>Locks in contracted pricing and triggers vendor manufacturing queue.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Vendor Ships (ASN)</strong></td>
                                <td>Inbound EDI 856 / Vendor API</td>
                                <td><code>In Stock / In Transit</code></td>
                                <td>Pre-populates serial numbers, MAC addresses, and carrier tracking before physical arrival.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Dock Scanned</strong></td>
                                <td>Agent Mobile Barcode Scan</td>
                                <td><code>In Stock / Available</code></td>
                                <td>Scan matches pre-staged ASN serials in under 2 seconds; zero manual typing errors.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Automated Pre-Imaging</strong></td>
                                <td>PXE Boot / Intune Autopilot / Jamf</td>
                                <td><code>In Stock / Staged</code></td>
                                <td>Serial number binds to Microsoft Autopilot hash; laptop configured automatically.</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

